What is std::scoped_lock?
std::scoped_lock is a C++17 RAII locking utility that can lock one or multiple mutexes simultaneously.

#include <mutex>

std::mutex m1;
std::mutex m2;

void foo()
{
    std::scoped_lock lock(m1, m2);

    // Both m1 and m2 are locked here.

    // Critical section
} // m2 and m1 are automatically unlocked

The key advantage is:
When locking multiple mutexes, std::scoped_lock uses a deadlock-avoidance algorithm so that competing threads don't deadlock simply because they acquire mutexes in different orders.

Why do we need it?
Consider a bank-transfer example.
We have two accounts:

struct Account {
    int balance;
    std::mutex m;
};


Suppose we transfer money:

void transfer(Account& from, Account& to, int amount)
{
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock1(from.m);
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock2(to.m);

    from.balance -= amount;
    to.balance += amount;
}

Looks reasonable.
But imagine two threads:
Thread 1
transfer(A, B, 100);

It does:
lock(A)
lock(B)

Thread 2
transfer(B, A, 50);

It does:

lock(B)
lock(A)

Possible execution:

Thread 1                  Thread 2
--------                  --------
lock(A)
                          lock(B)

wait for B                wait for A

       DEADLOCK

Neither thread can proceed.
This is the classic circular wait condition.

std::scoped_lock solves this
Instead:

void transfer(Account& from, Account& to, int amount)
{
    std::scoped_lock lock(from.m, to.m);

    from.balance -= amount;
    to.balance += amount;
}

Now C++ handles the acquisition of both mutexes in a deadlock-avoiding manner.
Even if we have:

transfer(A, B, 100);

and:

transfer(B, A, 50);

both calls use:
std::scoped_lock lock(...);

The library ensures that the mutexes are acquired without the simple lock-order deadlock described above.

What happens internally?
std::scoped_lock lock(m1, m2);

as being based on:
std::lock(m1, m2);

followed by RAII ownership.

Conceptually:

std::lock(m1, m2);

// Now both are locked

// scoped_lock remembers them

// destructor:
// unlock(m1)
// unlock(m2)

The important distinction is that std::lock() performs the deadlock-avoiding acquisition, while std::scoped_lock provides the RAII lifetime management.
You should not describe the implementation as literally being equivalent in every detail—the standard specifies behavior, not a particular implementation.

Staff-level example: thread-safe bank transfer
A good interview example is:

#include <iostream>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>

class Account {
public:
    explicit Account(int balance)
        : balance_(balance) {}

    int balance() const {
        return balance_;
    }

private:
    friend void transfer(Account&, Account&, int);

    int balance_;
    std::mutex mutex_;
};

void transfer(Account& from, Account& to, int amount)
{
    std::scoped_lock lock(from.mutex_, to.mutex_);

    if (from.balance_ < amount)
        return;

    from.balance_ -= amount;
    to.balance_ += amount;
}

int main()
{
    Account A(1000);
    Account B(1000);

    std::thread t1([&] {
        transfer(A, B, 100);
    });

    std::thread t2([&] {
        transfer(B, A, 200);
    });

    t1.join();
    t2.join();

    std::cout << A.balance() << '\n';
    std::cout << B.balance() << '\n';
}

Why this is a strong interview example
You can explain:
“The transfer operation needs to modify two independent objects atomically with respect to other transfers. Therefore I need to hold both mutexes simultaneously.
Acquiring them independently using two lock_guards can create lock-order inversion. scoped_lock allows me to acquire multiple mutexes using deadlock-avoidance and gives me RAII cleanup.”

scoped_lock vs lock_guard
This is a very common interview question.

| Feature |                                                             `lock_guard` |                                                        `scoped_lock` |
|---|---|---|
| Introduced                                                                 | C++11 |                                                          C++17 |
| RAII                                                                       | Yes |                                                             Yes |
| One mutex                                                                  | Yes |                                                             Yes |
| Multiple mutexes                                                           | No |                                                              **Yes** |
| Deadlock-safe multi-locking                                                | No |                                                               **Yes** |
| Unlock manually                                                            | No |                                                                 No |
| Moveable                                                                   | No |                                                                 No |
| `try_lock` support                                                         | No |                                                                 No |
| Typical use                                                                | Simple single mutex |                                           Multiple mutexes |


For multiple mutexes:
std::scoped_lock lock(m1, m2, m3);
is the natural C++17 solution.

Another subtle point: don't use adopt_lock unnecessarily
You may encounter:
std::mutex m1;
std::mutex m2;

std::lock(m1, m2);

std::lock_guard<std::mutex> l1(m1, std::adopt_lock);
std::lock_guard<std::mutex> l2(m2, std::adopt_lock);

This is older-style C++.
The idea is:

std::lock(m1, m2);

acquires both mutexes.
Then:
std::adopt_lock

tells lock_guard:
"The mutex is already locked. Take ownership of it."

C++17 gives us the much cleaner:
std::scoped_lock lock(m1, m2);

What about the same mutex twice?
Don't do:

std::scoped_lock lock(m, m);

For a normal non-recursive std::mutex, attempting to lock the same mutex multiple times is not a valid use of the API and can result 
in undefined behavior/deadlock depending on the locking operation and mutex type.
At Staff level, mention:

“The mutex arguments should represent distinct mutex objects unless the mutex type and operation explicitly support recursive locking semantics.”


                 Mutex ownership
                       │
          ┌────────────┼────────────┐
          │            │            │
     lock_guard   scoped_lock   unique_lock
          │            │            │
       Simple       Multiple      Flexible
       RAII          mutexes       control
          │            │            │
       m only       m1,m2,m3     lock/unlock
                                   wait()
                                   defer
                                   try_lock


The key interview insight is:
lock_guard = simple RAII ownership
scoped_lock = RAII + deadlock-safe acquisition of multiple mutexes
unique_lock = flexible/dynamic lock ownership


